--- !u!114 &214064271
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 214064269}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
    m_TypeName: UnityEngine.UI.MaskableGraphic+CullStateChangedEvent, UnityEngine.UI,
      Version=1.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null
  m_text: "[This entry was recovered from a damaged data tablet from an unknown Sergal
    researcher.]\r\n\r\n\tUpon first impressions the DL-111, or \"Boom Bunnies\" as
    the locals have taken to calling them,\r\nthey appear to be regular feral bunnies
    save for the \"fuse\" appendage that they have on their heads,\r\nhowever first
    impressions mean very little when it comes to the true nature of these creatures.\r\nAs
    we already know DL-111 is capable of detonating itself on command although not
    in any truly\r\npredictable manner making experiments with the subject particularly
    difficult however, there has been\r\na few learnings as far as conditions under
    which they decide not to explode. Anyone that has\r\ndealt with DL-111 up close
    will report one of three behavior patterns:\r\n\r\nPattern 1: Behavior pattern
    1 is synonymous with typical feral bunny behavior; scares easily, attempts\r\nto
    flee from confrontation, and will alert others of present or suspected danger.\r\nNo
    explosive tendencies recorded with this behavior pattern, even when cornered or
    captured.\r\nThis pattern has an occurance rate of roughly 25%\r\n\r\nPattern
    2: Behavior pattern 2 is notably more common than patterns 1 or 3. Pattern is
    characterized\r\nby a lack of fear and increased aggression, with DL-111 allowing
    potential threats to get significantly\r\ncloser before having any reactions.
    When a reaction is prompted reactions include being attacked\r\nby the provoked
    DL-111 and nearby DL-111 joining in to remove perceived threat.\r\nExplosive tendencies
    almost always recorded with this behavior pattern, except when captured, facing\r\na
    perceived overwhelming force, or pacified.\r\nThis pattern has an occurance rate
    of roughly 60%\r\n\r\nPattern 3: Behavior pattern 3 is rarely encountered and
    difficult to differentiate from pattern 2.\r\nSimilar to pattern 2, DL-111 displaying
    pattern 3 will allow potential threats to approach close, however\r\nwhen provocation
    distance is entered rather than becoming aggressive subjects will instead display\r\ncuriosity
    and approach the threat themselves docilely. Upon coming into direct contact with
    the possible\r\nthreat DL-111 will display uncharacteristic affection similar
    to a tamed creature even allowing themselves\r\nto be picked up and be handled.
    DL-111 displaying this pattern will remain docile unless the threat makes\r\nan
    active attack on the subject which will cause DL-111 to display actions like patterns
    1 or 2.\r\nNearby DL-111 will also display pattern 3 once direct contact is established.\r\nNo
    explosive tendencies recorded with this behavior pattern, even when captured.\r\nThis
    pattern has an occurance rate of roughly 15%\r\n\r\n\r\n\tDL-111 was supposedly
    first discovered in an immense grotto formed by several mountains that was seemingly\r\nclosed
    off from any other outside ecosystems save for any flying creatures that were
    able to fly in.\r\nDL-111 is suspected to have originated from this grotto then
    having spread across the realm over time.\r\n\r\n\tThe biology of DL-111 is not
    a simple one. Due to our limited technology in this realm, we were only able\r\nto
    identify so much. As mentioned previously DL-111 is capable of detonating on command
    but only under\r\nthe correct conditions, these conditions are believed to be
    as such based on our research and experiments.\r\nIf DL-111 is provoked or acting
    to defend a member of its species or equivalent, it can detonate, which explains\r\nwhy
    having one as a pet is so popular back at the empire, as they will throw themselves
    at any threat to their owner.\r\nIf DL-111 is in a state of substantial fear or
    safety, it will never detonate. If DL-111 is near the end of\r\nits life cycle
    it will never detonate. Experiments have also revealed that it's detonation strength
    is\r\nbased on its physical strength, which is suspected to be the reason why
    they are incapable of detonating\r\nwhen advanced in age. As we lack the necessary
    resources to find the exact reason as to what material\r\npresent in DL-111's
    biology allows DL-111 to detonate in the first place, we are able to extrapolate
    that\r\nit has something to do with water as the rest of DL-111's normal diet
    is exact to that of a normal bunny, minus\r\nits water intake rate being approximately
    3.5 times that of any other creature biologically similar to it. Although\r\nDL-111
    is capable of eating threats whole, this was observed to be a defence measure
    rather than a normal eating\r\nhabit as DL-111 seem to actively avoid this in
    most encounters. However the subject's control of its ability to\r\ndetonate to
    such an extent as to be able to \"explosively punch\" its targets with its paw
    shall remain unknown\r\nas we are unable to infer anything without advanced monitoring
    equipment.\r\n\r\n\tMilitary analysis by First Sergeant [Data corrupted]:\r\nBoom
    Bunnies are although deadly in hordes (or \"fluffles\" as the scientists keep
    correcting me), capable of being dealt with\r\nas such:\r\n\r\nHurt them quickly;
    don't let them have the chance to overwhelm you, if you weaken them, when they
    blow up it'll\r\nbe that much weaker of a blast.\r\n\r\nEat them; They're small
    and aren't that tough in most cases. When eaten they can't blow up. (Something
    to do with\r\nfear the scientists say)\r\n\r\nTrain your soldiers to be willing
    to face them; Although they're bunnies once your troops find out they can blow
    up\r\nit's a battle of 'will' at that point once the little buggers start throwing
    themselves at you. They\u2019re like missiles,\r\ntoo quick to rely on one's dexterity
    or agility, WILL POWER is the only thing protecting you at that point.\r\nThat
    or just put the toughest guys up front and let them absorb the blasts.\r\n\r\nRange
    is your friend; Like with most feral monsters. Need I explain further?\r\n\r\n[End
    of log.]\r"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
