--- !u!114 &1434798153
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1434798151}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
    m_TypeName: UnityEngine.UI.MaskableGraphic+CullStateChangedEvent, UnityEngine.UI,
      Version=1.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null
  m_text: "Anonymous notes on the Aabayx:\r\n\r\nOf all the peoples that inhabit this
    world, the Aabayx are probably the strangest out there. Their \"Empire,\" referred
    to as the \"Followers of the 20th Sythkrast,\" only emerged, seemingly out of
    nowhere, a few short years ago, and ever since, they\u2019ve proven to be\u2026
    Difficult to learn anything about. However, by speaking with an Aabayx who had
    surrendered and defected from their nation, I was able to gain an insight into
    these deeply strange and frankly unpleasant people.\r\n\r\nFor a start they are
    highly religious, worshiping a pantheon of many gods, each one being assigned
    to a number. Their main god, \u201CYsynsr aaz Lextrnl,\u201D is supposed to be
    the \u201Cdivine manifestation\u201D of the number 20, with that number being
    of great importance to them. Their religion is also very Aabayx centric, with
    them labeling all \u201CAtzaxats\u201D, their word for anything that isn\u2019t
    an Aabayx, as inferior and only deserving of being food or slaves. If I had to
    sum them up in one word, in fact, I\u2019d probably sum them up as \u201CZealots.\u201D\r\n\r\nAnother
    thing to note is that before the so called \u201C20th Sythkrast\u201D started,
    the Aabayx were mostly nomads, but a bit over 2000 years ago, some of them began
    preparing for the coming 20th Sythkrast. When it started, they finished up and
    founded their modern empire. They have no concept of a separate religion and government,
    they don\u2019t even have separate words for the two.\r\n\r\nNow, one thing that
    you may notice about these things, if you encounter one, is that they have an
    exoskeleton. Their outside is mostly solid, with a truly bizarre texture, though
    the central body is a bit more flexible, it\u2019s still pretty solid. Because
    of their hard bodies, the Aabayx seem to have a preference towards weapons designed
    to crush. To that extent, one of the main melee weapons they use is a giant warhammer
    thing they call \u201Cpersonbreakers.\u201D\r\n\r\nThey do have other weapons
    though. Firstly are the \u201Clongscalpels,\u201D basically swords, and poorly
    made ones. An Aabayx with a personbreaker is one that is considered important
    in some way. One with a longscalpel is viewed as expendable. I got to hold a longscalpel,
    actually. Surprisingly sharp, but really flimsy and bendable, and it doesn\u2019t
    feel like it would make for a good sword. But then they also have bows.\r\n\r\nWith
    only one eye, the Aabayx lack depth perception, but when they do learn to use
    ranged weapons, they can be shockingly accurate. But doing so takes a lot of extra
    work for them to learn in the first place, so they value those among them who
    can use bows quite highly, and their bows are always decorated to at least some
    extent from what I\u2019ve seen.\r\n\r\nNow, moving to other topics, it\u2019s
    been \nbserved that the Aabayx can only eat still living prey, dead prey or regular,
    non-living food being indigestible to them. I\u2019ve asked the Aabayx I know
    why and they don\u2019t seem to-\r\n\r\n(The piece of paper is torn and is cut
    off here, the rest of whatever was written being lost.)"
