    m_TypeName: UnityEngine.UI.MaskableGraphic+CullStateChangedEvent, UnityEngine.UI,
      Version=1.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=null
  m_text: "Foreword\r\nI will begin my representation by introducing myself and telling
    how I came by the information I am now forwarding to you, my esteemed readers.\r\n\nMy
    name is H. Avry, an explorer and scholar of all things that live and breed. Having
    in my youth learned most of what could be learned about the common habits of animals
    inhabiting Britain and western Europe, I proceeded to study the habits less often
    observed and studied, largely the breeding habits of wild animals, along with
    the related organs.\r\n\nThis field, I soon learned, didn't yield me overt respect
    in the academic circles. That may have been why my attempts to get a grant or
    sponsorship for research trips further from Britain kept failing. So I was quite
    pleasantly surprised when a way to explore new lands, lands very exotic indeed,
    came upon me without any previous sign.\r\n\nWithin my very house, in the cellar
    between the wine rack and the pickle shelf, there was a shimmering... something.
    Through this misty portal, I could see glimmers of a strange land, inhabited by
    creatures unlike those I was familiar with, often unlike anything I had ever even
    heard of! For the first few hours I kept carefully observing everything I saw
    within the mist, making notes and quick sketches of my observations, when it came
    upon me that the phenomena seemed to be growing dimmer, and lessening in size
    and clarity.\r\nThere was no choice I could see. I immediately gathered everything
    that could be of use to me on unknown lands, gathering everything in my cellar.
    I know not how I knew that I could pass through the mist, but know I did, though
    I threw the first of my bags through the portal and watched it settle among the
    grasses before I took hold of the remaining bags and ran at the mist.\r\n\nI felt
    nauseated for a while, but that soon passed. Looking around, I saw a village in
    the distance, a stroke of luck even greater than I initially thought. To my surprise
    I found I could understand the language the villagers spoke, heavily accented
    though it was,  something I would not have believed possible seeing the way the
    locals dressed, or often, didn't dress. From them I learned much of the world
    I found myself in, and more importantly, was able to hire a guide and guards to
    accompany me, the first of many I would end up picking up. I also exhanged most
    of my gear for local versions, the villagers paid me well for the \"rare\" items
    I had brought with me. Really, aside from the money, I only kept with me my trusty
    helmet, my notebooks and a selection of pencils, everything else the locals could
    provide sufficient alternatives for.\r\n\nThe rest is best told alongside the
    description of the creatures and phenomena which presence I experienced the events
    in.\r\nH. Avry"
